'''''Sonchus''''' is a genus of flowering plants in the tribe [[Cichorieae]] within the family [[Asteraceae]]<ref name=u>{{Tropicos|40001998|Sonchus|L.}}</ref><ref>[https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/358814#page/235/mode/1up Linnaeus, Carl von. 1753. Species Plantarum 2: 793-795] in Latin</ref> and are commonly known as '''sow thistles''' (less commonly '''hare thistles''' or '''hare lettuces'''). Sowthistles are annual, biennial or perennial herbs, with or without rhizomes and a few are even woody (subgenus ''Dendrosonchus,'' restricted to the [[Canary Islands]] and [[Madeira]]).<ref>For a recent review of woody species, see Seung-Chul Kim et al. (1996). "A common origin for woody ''Sonchus'' and five related genera in the Macaronesian islands: Molecular evidence for extensive radiation." Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA, 93:7743-7748.</ref><ref>{{EFloras|2|130683|Sonchus |first1=Zhu |last1=Shi |first2=Norbert |last2=Kilian |volume=20&ndash;21}}</ref><ref>[http://luirig.altervista.org/flora/taxa/gen.php?genere=Sonchus Altervista Flora Italiana genere Sonchus] photos and distribution maps for several species</ref><ref>[http://bie.ala.org.au/species/urn:lsid:biodiversity.org.au:apni.taxon:308696 Atlas of Living Australia]</ref><ref>[http://apps.kew.org/efloras/namedetail.do?qry=namelist&taxon=5022&nameid=12147 Flora Zambesiaca]</ref>

==Description==
[[File:Cerrajón herreño (Sonchus hierrensis), La Gomera, España, 2012-12-14, DD 01.jpg|thumb|left|''Sonchus hierrensis'' in the Canary Island of La Gomera]]
The genus is named after the [[Ancient Greece|Ancient Greek]] for such plants, {{Lang|grc|σόγχος}}. All are characterized by soft, somewhat irregularly lobed [[leaves]] that clasp the stem and, at least initially, form a basal rosette. The [[Plant stem|stem]] contains a milky [[latex]]. [[Head (botany)|Flower heads]] are yellow and range in size from half to one inch in diameter; the [[flower|florets]] are all of ray type. ''Sonchus'' fruits are single-seeded, dry and [[Dehiscence_(botany)|indehiscent]].<ref>{{Cite book|title=Vascular plant taxonomy|last=R.|first=Walters, Dirk|date=2006|publisher=Kendall/Hunt Pub. Co|others=Keil, David J., Murrell, Zack E.|isbn=0757512143|edition= 5th |location=Dubuque, IA|oclc=62889410}}</ref> Sow thistles are common roadside plants, and while native to [[Eurasia]] and tropical [[Africa]], they are found almost worldwide in [[temperate]] regions.<ref>{{EFloras|1|130683|Sonchus |first=Philip E. |last=Hyatt |tribe=Cichorieae}}</ref>

Mature sow thistle stems can range from 30&nbsp;cm to 2&nbsp;m (1 to 6&nbsp;ft) tall, depending upon species and growing conditions. Coloration ranges from green to purple in older plants. Sow thistles exude a milky [[latex]] when any part of the plant is cut or damaged, and it is from this fact that the plants obtained the common name, "sow thistle", as they were fed to lactating [[pig|sows]] in the belief that [[milk]] production would increase. Sow thistles are known as "milk thistles" in some regions, although [[milk thistle]] more commonly refers to the genus ''[[Silybum]]''.

=== Comparison with dandelions ===
They superficially resemble and are in the same family as [[Taraxacum|dandelions]], and even have [[Pappus (botany)|pappus]]-like structures resembling dandelion seeds. However, they are usually taller than dandelions, have multiple flowers per stem (as opposed to one flower per dandelion), and have more irregularly shaped lobes.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bergo |first=Alan |date=2024-08-31 |title=Foraging Sow Thistle: Identification, Look Alikes and Uses |url=https://foragerchef.com/sow-thistle/ |access-date=2025-06-02 |website=Forager {{!}} Chef |language=en-US}}</ref>

